On October 9th 2026 we got hit with the following announcement:

- On Deno's blog: [https://deno.com/blog/cloudflare](https://deno.com/blog/cloudflare)
- On Cloudflare's blog: [https://blog.cloudflare.com/deno-joins-cloudflare/](https://blog.cloudflare.com/deno-joins-cloudflare/)

First thing I would like to get out of the way is that I am perfectly aware that few people were as emotionally invested in Deno as a project as I was, mainly because **Deno 1** never really took off and that diminished the general public's exposure to how great **Deno 2** was (notice the past tense), fine, it doesn't matter that it never reached widespread adoption, many do agree it was great quality software and a good direction for the JS/TS ecosystem.

## The Announcement

In Deno's own blog post, which is a rather short 2min read time statement, we can find the following quote:

> Joining also means choosing where to focus our effort. We’ve decided to put our future development work into this shared platform rather than continuing to develop a separate runtime and hosting service. We know this is a consequential change for people who have built on Deno.

Everyone is entitled to their own opinion regarding software ecosystems and plurality, this entire post is subjective from my part, but that precise quote is the part I find most concerning for a number of reasons.
